/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  landscape01_ammonia_synthesis
  =============================

  THE AMMONIA LANDSCAPE -- N2 + 3 H2 at stoichiometric feed, equilibrium
  NH3 mole fraction against temperature at 1, 10 and 100 bar (the ideal-gas
  element-potential kernel, the same one nh3_equilibrium_map contours over
  the whole plane).  This is the first witness of the EduTool
  `equilibrium-landscapes` (gui/src/ui/methods/EquilibriumLandscapesTool.tsx):
  a SURVEY over reaction systems the engine already solves, read live from
  this case's `map.csv` and its anchor KPIs.

  WHAT THE THREE CURVES SHOW.  Exothermic AND mole-reducing (4 -> 2), so the
  equilibrium loves COLD and PRESSURE: every curve falls with T, and the
  100 bar curve sits above the 1 bar one wherever the gas is dry.  The grid
  starts at 300 K on purpose: from 300 to 380 K the 100 bar cells are a
  SATURATED gas -- the kernel finds NH3 supersaturated there and condenses
  it (ElementPotential.cpp, the one-condensable two-phase branch), and the
  mole fraction it reports is the GAS-phase Psat(T)/P.  That is why the
  100 bar curve RISES with T at its cold end: that leg is ammonia's vapour
  pressure, not its chemistry, and the curve joins the dry equilibrium at
  the dew point near 400 K (measured: 0.1047 at 300 K, 0.9311 at 400 K).

  THE CONTRAST THE PAGE CLOSES ON, computed here and never typed.  Two anchor
  cells:  298.15 K / 1 bar -- ambient, where the reaction is thermodynamically
  favourable and nothing in this tree can make it go (the only ammonia rate
  law here, Dyson & Simon 1968, REFUSES a pressure below 150 atm by name:
  src/unitOperations/reactor/kinetics/AmmoniaSynthesisRate.cpp,
  `validityWindow`) -- and 700 K / 200 bar, the gibbs10_ammonia_fugacity
  operating point, which this ideal-gas kernel answers as the SHAPE and that
  SRK case answers with the fugacity correction folded in.  The third anchor,
  700 K / 1 bar, is what the same chemistry reaches at converter temperature
  without the pressure.

  Anchor KPI names carry the approach magnitude when one is declared
  (`metric_T25C_P1atm_dT<n>`), so a changed approach breaks the golden loudly.
  Nothing here is validated against a measurement: the Larson & Dodge (1923)
  pins live in nh3_equilibrium_map and are not repeated.

  WHY THE PRESSURES ARE 1, 10 AND 100 BAR.  `Pgrid` is `from / to / n / log`
  (GibbsMapOp.cpp reads no list form), and three log-spaced points from 1 to
  100 bar land on the decades a textbook draws.  Ideal-gas validity is the
  same caveat as nh3_equilibrium_map's: the high-pressure curve is the
  SHAPE of the pressure dependence, not a quantitative prediction.

  temperatureApproach IS DECLARED, AT ZERO.  The EduTool's slider replaces
  the NUMBER of a declared scalar and can add no key (gui/src/case/
  methodRun.ts), so the key must be here for the page to move it.  Zero is
  true equilibrium and prints nothing; a magnitude > 0 makes the run assign
  the sign at EVERY cell from the thermicity of the overall transformation
  at that cell's T (GibbsMapOp.cpp, `resolveDirection`) and print the tally.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name      ammonia;
        type      gibbsMap;
        elements  ( N H );
        species
        (
            { name N2;    atoms ( 2 0 ); }
            { name H2;    atoms ( 0 2 ); }
            { name NH3;   atoms ( 1 3 ); }
        );
        feed      { N2 1.0;  H2 3.0; }
        Tgrid     { from 300; to 1000; n 36; }             // K
        Pgrid     { from 1.0e5; to 1.0e7; n 3; log true; }  // Pa: 1, 10, 100 bar
        temperatureApproach 0;                          // K, a MAGNITUDE; 0 = true equilibrium
        metric    { type moleFraction; species NH3; }
        anchors
        (
            { T 298.15;   P 1.0e5;   }   // ambient: 25 C, 1 bar -- the contrast page's first point
            { T 700;      P 2.0e7;   }   // 700 K, 200 bar -- gibbs10_ammonia_fugacity's point, ideal-gas here
            { T 700;      P 1.0e5;   }   // 700 K, 1 bar -- converter temperature without the pressure
        );
        output    { file map.csv; }
    }
);
